WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Jamming or strong interference can reduce signal quality or prevent a receiver from

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.765
maintaining satellite lock.

00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:09.380
Possible effects include fewer usable satellites, poor accuracy estimates,

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.800
navigation warnings, delayed position updates, or loss of position-dependent flight modes.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.640
The aircraft may drift or hold position less reliably when it transitions away from

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.445
satellite-assisted stabilization.

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:23.010
Automated missions can pause, abort, or behave according to a failsafe.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.090
The exact response depends on the aircraft and configuration.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:30.325
Unexpected return behavior or a navigation-mode change deserves immediate attention.

00:00:30.325 --> 00:00:33.740
Read warnings, maintain visual awareness, and reduce mission complexity.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.650
Do not dismiss an alert because the map still looks plausible.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.920
Similar symptoms can have non-malicious causes.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.670
Buildings, terrain, foliage, antenna orientation, reflected signals,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.260
weather-related obstruction, power infrastructure, damaged equipment,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.160
or software faults can degrade reception.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.650
Localized radio noise from faulty or incompatible equipment can also resemble intentional

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:53.420
interference.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.035
Note whether the problem follows one aircraft, one controller, one location,

00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:59.920
or multiple independent systems before drawing conclusions.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.500
Look for a pattern across multiple indicators: satellite count or status,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.040
quality estimates, map behavior, visual position, flight-mode changes,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.480
compass information, and the physical environment.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.685
A single changing number remains weak evidence.

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:13.480
The safety response is conservative.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.385
Stop automated collection, keep the aircraft in sight, avoid flying farther,

00:01:17.385 --> 00:01:20.610
and return or land using trained procedures before battery, signal,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.144
or orientation margins narrow.

00:01:22.144 --> 00:01:25.440
Diagnose cause after the aircraft and people are safe.
